      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "| $x$ | **$0$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$6$** | **$8$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n\nConsider the data above and the matrices below. Find an expression for the vector of coefficients of the curve $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}$ that best fits the data. \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & {0}^{2} & {0}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 2 & {2}^{2} & {2}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 4 & {4}^{2} & {4}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 6 & {6}^{2} & {6}^{3} \\\\ 1 & 8 & {8}^{2} & {8}^{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\beta =[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1} \\\\ {\\beta }_{2} \\\\ {\\beta }_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The coefficients of the curve \n\n$$\ny={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x+{\\beta }_{2}{x}^{2}+{\\beta }_{3}{x}^{3}\n$$\n\nthat best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\nX\\beta =y,\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$",
